WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.310 align:start position:0%
 
now the trade union congress is going to  hold a rally in london next month  calling for mass pay rises for workers  because of the cost of living crisis now  there were hints this week that the  government could do more possibly tax  cuts even the emergency budget that  labour's been calling for but if there  isn't more action could the unions be  squaring up for a summer of strikes well  we're joined now by the general  secretary of the tuc francis o'grady  thank you so much for being with us how  worried are you about the rising cost of  living and what that means for low-paid  workers in particular  i think everybody's worried they're  seeing  those price increases eating to pay  packets but also into pensions and  benefits and i think there's a real  sense that  the government needs to get on with this  now you know we know that millions of  working families are struggling we're  seeing the number of children in  absolute poverty increase uh electricity  and gas bills rising at 10 times the  rate of wages it's about time that the  government recognizes a living standards  emergency and they need to come back to  parliament with an emergency budget i  mean frankly there's so much talk on the  conservative back benches that we're  going to see another u-turn on this i  wish the government would just get on  with it  you i want to see more rises in people's  pay to help them at a time of course  when the cost of living is increasing  but is there a concern that that could  further stoke inflation andrew bailey  the governor of the bank of england has  said you know we do need to see a  moderation of wage rises and i don't  want to sugarcoat that it's painful but  we need to see that in order to get  through this problem more quickly do you  agree with that  england was wrong on this one the  governor was wrong uh all the evidence  is that prices are being driven by  increases in energy prices certainly not  wages which are set to fall in real  terms on the contrary people need help  now  and in addition if we suck demand out of  the economy then we're going to risk  that recession  uh coming ever sooner so what we need is  for the chancellor who i'm afraid  woefully failed working families with  his spring statement and i think  most people recognize that he needs to  come back with that whimful tax on the  energy companies that would provide some  immediate relief a real investment in  renewables so we tackle the root causes  of the problems with high energy prices  a boost in the minimum wage how on earth  people are supposed to survive on nine  pounds 50 an hour when in real terms of  course that's set to fall we need a a  real boost the minimum wage and fair pay  agreements across the board and and the  chancellor you know we've had enough of  lessons from conservative mps on  cooking or buying value brands i think  they are looking you know incredibly out  of touch with what most people are  experiencing people on low incomes are  very good at managing their budgets the  problem is those budgets are too tight  and of course it's bad for the country  too we've we've seen this government  fail to bring in an employment rights  bill to tackle low pay and insecurity  and that is costing the exchequer to the  tune of 10 billion pounds  in lost tax revenue and higher benefit  payouts it's bad economics it's a false  economy  and working and respect governments to  stand up for them and help them now not  in a few months time when it's too late  over the last 12 months the tec has  logged 300 disputes in different  industries which effectively illustrates  that people are challenging below  inflation pay rises  do you think we could see widespread  industrial action if more isn't done on  pay  look i think the government needs to  start listening to people and get back  in touch with the strength of feeling on  this  um yes of course unions exist to try it  and get better and fairer pay deals and  terms and conditions for their members  that's what they do and we have seen  that mood increasing in both the public  and the private sector  but um of course we've got that national  demonstration on saturday the 8th the  18th of june  where working people will be coming  together to protest and demanding better  but you know the way to deal with this  is for  the chancellor to get a move on come  back to parliament with that emergency  budget for employers to get around the  table with unions to negotiate fair  solutions we've seen profits recover  very well by the way i think um the  governor did belatedly acknowledge that  so that was the case  and we just need a bit of fair treatment  you know we we all want more money for  our public services we all want the  country's finances to be in good health  but it's a case of fairness here and i  think people feel very strongly that uh  the wealthy are not paying their fair  share of tax  that workers are being treated like a  cash machine hammered every time there's  a crisis we've had 10 years of this  don't forget over 10 years of austerity  pay freezes real pay cuts  and there's there's no more fat left to  trim  people are looking for help now  at the same time you know you mentioned  there there the rally coming up and  ahead of that rally your colleague dave  ward the general secretary of the  communication workers union has said  employers are out of control  i mean most employers are businesses who  are also struggling with the rising cost  of living aren't they like the rising  cost of of everything from energy bills  to  products and supply at the supply side  they're providing jobs for millions of  people is that really the kind of  language that is helpful  look i think there's clearly a mixed  picture um you know we have some very  good agreements with employers and we've  made some very good um pay settlements  but we've also got employers who were  hoarding cash who were paying fairly um  i think most people would agree obscene  increases in top pay in the boardroom  and you know as i say people just want  to see a bit of fairness but i think the  government can't walk away from this  i think most people agree that that  spring statement which included tax  hikes  which hit the lowest paid  and young workers hardest on national  insurance you know simply wasn't fair  they need to come back with an emergency  budget but rather than spinning it out  to the  summer or august as we're hearing in  some newspapers they need to come back  and do it now there are some ready-made  solutions that make sense including that  windfall tax  on  energy companies and let's not forget  that the oil companies said it would  make no difference at all to their plans  for investment they can afford it  and they should do it and the government  should get on with it  now i do want to ask you about the prime  minister's comments about working from  home i asked quasi-costa about them  early he said that he thought that  actually for some people it did reflect  their experience  the prime minister said my experience of  working from home is that you spend an  awful lot of time making another cup of  coffee and then you know getting up  walking slowly to the fridge hacking off  a small piece of cheese and then walking  very slowly back to your laptop and  forgetting what it was you were doing  do you think he's right  well it feels a bit ironic given i guess  the prime minister is the classic case  of somebody who always works from home  because he's based in number 10 and that  may be his experience  of grabbing coffees and wandering around  the kitchen but i think  again people deserve a bit of basic  respect i think people who work from  home work very hard  and this should be a matter you know for  unions and employers to sit down and  come up with sensible agreements i i  would have hoped that politicians facing  a living standards emergency had more  important things to be getting on with  than trying to interfere in day-to-day  industrial relations between civil  servants  managers and their unions  this week the government also announced  90 000 job losses among civil servants  and i think it's important to say you  know we're not just talking about what  the newspapers would call sort of pen  pushers  these are prison officers court staff  customs officers  immigration case handlers  are you worried that this is going to  damage public services and specifically  in what ways  well of course this this is you know  back to austerity and we saw how  austerity failed not only ordinary  people and families but it failed  uh the country in the end by holding  back growth and of course you know these  these are key workers who work right  through the crisis  uh important uh public services for all  of us that provide that safety net  uh that keep us safe  you know these are important jobs so how  on earth the government expects to be  able to shed 90 000 at a stroke and for  it not to  damage  communities in the country i i really  don't know so i think we need to  hear from them exactly what their plans  are but don't be surprised  that unions are angry and that  communities will be extremely angry if  we're looking to get hit again in terms  of  key public services that keep us safe  and just finally um you are stepping  down at the end of the year after a  decade as the first woman to hold the  job of tuc general secretary and i've  spoken to you many times you know over  that period and i'm just wondering you  know if there's any kind of reflections  you would give  on that decade in the job you know do  you think workers are better off now or  when you started and also what has it  been like for being  like a woman you know in the world of  work right at that moment and at a time  when obviously more recently there's  been lots of debate about um women in  the workplace as well  well i'm going to be keeping my  shoulders to the wheel for some time yet  i don't leave until the end of december  and i'm focused absolutely on you know  doing our bit uh to ensure that the  government and employers  face head on this living standards  crisis and give working people a fair  deal  um but i am proud of what we've achieved  and i think the real lesson is that when  we work together as a trade union  movement we can achieve a lot if you  think about the pandemic our wonderful  uh members in health social care  transport  refuse collection right across the piece  but so often they had to look to unions  to protect their health and safety they  didn't have proper ppe  we had to fight for the national  guidelines  and of course the furlough scheme  wouldn't have happened without the trade  union movement and you know ultimately  protected 12 million livelihoods at its  peak and  you know that  was designed to help the economy recover  more quickly too but you know to do that  in a way that channeled support through  people's pay packets is the best way to  sustain  demands in in local economies shops and  businesses so  you know i think we've got a lot to be  proud of on the equality front um you  know we are now a movement 50 50 men and  women uh we reflect  all society and every race every  religion every background and i'm proud  of that too because i think when working  people stand together when we're united  we can win  okay thank you so much for being on the  program always interesting to talk fans  are so great there  thank you

